{"study_accession":"PRJNA544266","secondary_study_accession":"SRP199210","title":"How Kaposi’s sarcoma-associated herpesvirus stably transforms peripheral B cells towards Lymphomagenesis","description":"Primary Effusion Lymphomas (PELs) are causally associated with Kaposi's sarcoma-associated herpesvirus (KSHV) and 90% of PELs are co-infected with Epstein-Barr virus (EBV). Understanding how PELs develop has been impaired by the difficulty of infecting B cells with KSHV in vitro, and the inability of KSHV to transform them. We show that EBV supports an optimal co-infection of 2.5% of peripheral B cells by KSHV. We demonstrate for the first time that dually infected B cells are stably transformed in vitro and show that while both viruses can be maintained, different cells exhibit distinct, transformed properties. Transformed cells that grow to predominate in a culture express increased levels of most KSHV genes and differentially express a subset of cellular genes as do bona fide PEL cells.","pubmed":"","geo_series":"","n_samples":12,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN11831225","study_accession":"PRJNA544266","blood_class":"blood_cells","class_from":"sample","title":"Human sample from Homo sapiens","description":"Human sample from Homo sapiens","sex":"not applicable","geo_sample":"","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2019-07-11","attributes":[["isolate","not applicable"],["age","not applicable"],["biomaterial_provider","Sugden Lab, 1111 Highland Ave., Madison, WI USA"],["sex","not applicable"],["tissue","B-lymphocyte"],["population","1"],["sample identifier","P1_1"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR9108926","experiment_accession":"SRX5883427","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"RANDOM","instrument_model":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000","read_count":53609250,"base_count":16189993500,"first_public":"2019-07-11","experiment_title":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000 sequencing: RNA-Seq of peripheral B cells infected with KSHV and EBV"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":f